= Computational complexity — Simple =

'''Computational complexity''' studies how much time, memory, or other resources are needed to solve a problem as the input gets larger.

A problem can be possible to compute but still take so much time that it is not practical.

Complexity often focuses on how fast the required work grows.

For example, an algorithm whose work doubles with every extra input item can become unusable much faster than one whose work grows roughly linearly.

Complexity is not the same as measuring how many seconds one program takes on one computer.

